1. with same IP/Domain
2. With seperate ip(servers)/Domains


1. Use relative path (recommended)

If your frontend and backend are hosted on the same domain/server, just use:

const API_BASE = '/api';


Then configure your reverse proxy (like Nginx or Apache) to forward /api requests to the backend (e.g., Node.js at port 3000).

Example Nginx config:

location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://localhost:3000/api/;
}

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Use full server URL (if backend is separate)

If frontend and backend are on different servers:

const API_BASE = 'http://<your-ec2-public-ip>:3000/api';


But then make sure:

Your EC2 security group allows inbound traffic on port 3000.

The backend listens on 0.0.0.0 (not just localhost).

app.listen(3000, '0.0.0.0', () => console.log('Server running'));


You handle CORS on the backend:

const cors = require('cors');
app.use(cors());

🔍 Quick way to check

In your browser console (Network tab), see what URL the frontend is calling.
If it says http://localhost:3000/... while the page is hosted on your EC2 IP — that’s the problem.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------